    <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-semibold leading-tight mb-4" style="color: var(--text-primary);">How to Compress Images for Indian Government Forms (SSC, IBPS, UPSC, PAN, Aadhaar)</h1>

    <div class="prose prose-gray max-w-none">

      <p>Every Indian government portal — SSC, IBPS, UPSC, NSDL, UIDAI — rejects your photo or signature the moment it exceeds the file size limit. The fix takes under 30 seconds: <a href="/compress/for-government">compress the image to the exact KB range the portal requires</a>, without destroying clarity. This guide gives you the exact specs for every major portal and shows you how to meet them using Filekrush.</p>

      <h2>Why Government Portals Reject Your Uploads</h2>

      <p>A modern smartphone camera produces photos between 3 MB and 8 MB. Government portals cap photo uploads at 50 KB — sometimes as low as 20 KB. That's a 100x to 400x reduction from what your phone generates.</p>

      <p>The portals don't tell you this clearly. You get a vague error like "file size exceeds the limit" or, worse, the upload button simply does nothing. No explanation, no suggested fix.</p>

      <p>This isn't a design flaw — it's an infrastructure constraint. When crores of candidates apply for SSC CGL or IBPS PO simultaneously, the servers need lightweight files. But the burden of meeting those specs falls entirely on the applicant.</p>

      <p>I'm the IT person in my family. Whenever relatives — including government employees — need help with form uploads or submissions, it's a bad day for me. When I married a lawyer, my responsibility multiplied, because she had to do forms and uploads on a daily basis for her work. That's why I built Filekrush.</p>

